"""Original oncologic emergencies: spinal cord and superior vena cava compression."""
from create_oncology_tls import rows,q,FN,NS,TL,RA,HC,ref
MS=ref('NICE metastatic spinal cord compression guideline','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng234/chapter/Recommendations')
SV=ref('NCI cardiopulmonary syndromes: SVC obstruction','https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/cardiopulmonary-hp-pdq')

q('A patient with cancer has new progressive back pain, bilateral leg weakness and urinary dysfunction. What investigation is required urgently?', 'Whole-spine MRI for suspected metastatic spinal cord compression',[
 ('Plain radiographs alone as an adequate exclusion test','They cannot exclude epidural cord compression.'),('Wait for complete paraplegia before imaging','Delay can cause irreversible loss of function.'),('Image only the painful lumbar segment regardless of neurological findings','Multiple levels can occur and whole-spine assessment is important.')],
 'Preserve neurological function before it is lost','**Cancer with new neurological signs and back pain is a cord-compression emergency.**',['Use the urgent MSCC pathway and assess spinal stability.','NICE recommends MRI as soon as possible and within twenty-four hours for suspected MSCC.'],'**Coordinate steroids, safe movement and definitive treatment planning.**',['Do not delay urgent care for routine outpatient investigations.','Assess baseline walking and bladder/bowel function.'],[['Red flags','Back pain plus neurological dysfunction'],['Imaging','Urgent whole-spine MRI'],['Goal','Prevent irreversible deficit']],[MS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Under NICE guidance, an adult with suspected MSCC and neurological symptoms should generally receive which initial corticosteroid approach while definitive treatment is arranged?', 'Dexamethasone 16 mg orally or an equivalent parenteral dose promptly, followed by the recommended daily plan',[
 ('No steroid until irreversible paraplegia is present','Neurological symptoms warrant prompt treatment.'),('Routine 96 mg daily for every patient regardless of toxicity','Very high doses are not the standard universal approach.'),('A single low topical steroid application','This does not address epidural/cord edema.')],
 'Reduce edema while arranging definitive decompression or tumor treatment','**Dexamethasone is an adjunct rather than definitive mechanical treatment.**',['Monitor glucose and provide indicated GI protection.','Subsequent dosing and taper depend on the treatment plan and diagnosis.'],'**Seek immediate specialist coordination.**',['Suspected lymphoma without neurological symptoms requires particular care before steroids because biopsy can be affected.','Do not let medication delay imaging or surgical/radiation assessment.'],[['Initial adult approach','16 mg dexamethasone/equivalent'],['Role','Edema reduction'],['Definitive care','Surgery/radiotherapy/systemic plan']],[MS])

q('A CT shows no obvious destructive vertebral lesion, but a cancer patient has a progressive spinal sensory level and weakness. Which conclusion is correct?', 'Cord compression or another myelopathy remains possible and urgent MRI is needed when feasible',[
 ('Normal bony CT excludes every epidural lesion','Soft-tissue and neural disease may not be excluded.'),('The symptoms must be peripheral neuropathy','A sensory level and pyramidal features indicate a cord concern.'),('A normal CT makes neurological progression safe to observe without escalation','The clinical syndrome still requires urgent action.')],
 'A test for bone does not fully answer a neural-compression question','**MRI evaluates the cord, epidural tissues and multiple levels.**',['Use an appropriate alternative pathway if MRI is contraindicated.','Clinical deterioration matters even before definitive imaging.'],'**Continue the emergency MSCC assessment.**',['Protect an unstable spine according to expert advice.','Review other inflammatory or vascular cord causes when appropriate.'],[['CT','Important but not universal exclusion'],['Neurological level','Cord concern'],['Next step','Urgent appropriate neural imaging']],[MS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Which factors guide surgery versus radiotherapy or combined treatment for MSCC?', 'Neurological status, mechanical stability, tumor biology, extent, prognosis and patient goals',[
 ('One treatment is automatically best for every metastatic lesion','Selection is individualized.'),('Only the histological name matters and spinal instability is irrelevant','Instability can require a mechanical solution.'),('Loss of walking is the only relevant outcome','Pain, stability, continence, survival and goals also matter.')],
 'The emergency has mechanical and oncological components','**Radiotherapy sensitivity does not itself correct every unstable spine.**',['Selected patients benefit from decompression/stabilization.','Fitness and expected benefit influence proportionality.'],'**Use urgent multidisciplinary planning.**',['Do not delay because the primary cancer is not yet fully staged.','Communicate uncertainty and functional priorities.'],[['Neurology','Current function and progression'],['Mechanics','Stability'],['Oncology/person','Biology, prognosis and goals']],[MS])

q('A patient with a mediastinal mass has facial swelling, distended upper-chest veins and bilateral arm edema, worse when supine. Which syndrome is most likely?', 'Superior vena cava obstruction',[
 ('Isolated lower-limb venous insufficiency','The upper-body distribution indicates a different venous territory.'),('A nephrotic edema pattern uniquely established by the face','The chest collaterals and arm findings support central venous obstruction.'),('Portal hypertension as the direct explanation for upper-body congestion','Its typical collateral and fluid pattern differs.')],
 'Venous distribution helps localize obstruction','**Impaired SVC drainage congests the head, neck and upper limbs.**',['Tumor compression and thrombosis are important causes.','Rate of development and collateral flow influence severity.'],'**Assess airway, cerebral and hemodynamic compromise urgently.**',['Obtain appropriate contrast imaging when suitable.','Define the cause before a definitive disease-specific plan.'],[['Territory','Head/neck/arms'],['Clues','Chest collaterals/supine worsening'],['Causes','Compression or thrombosis']],[SV],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A stable patient has suspected malignant SVC obstruction without stridor, cerebral compromise or shock. What diagnostic principle is appropriate?', 'Obtain an appropriate tissue diagnosis before empirical tumor-directed treatment when feasible',[
 ('Give immediate radiation to every mediastinal mass before diagnosis','Histology can change treatment and empirical therapy can obscure it.'),('All SVC obstruction requires emergency surgery regardless of severity','Treatment depends on cause and clinical urgency.'),('A normal oxygen saturation proves no evaluation is needed','Venous obstruction still requires assessment.')],
 'Urgency should reflect physiology rather than the label alone','**Not every SVC obstruction has the same immediate threat.**',['Lymphoma, lung cancer and thrombosis need different definitive strategies.','Biopsy planning should minimize risk and preserve yield.'],'**Coordinate prompt cause-specific care.**',['Escalate immediately if airway or cerebral symptoms emerge.','Use symptom relief and positioning as appropriate while investigating.'],[['Stable syndrome','Prompt diagnosis and plan'],['Histology','Treatment-defining'],['Severe compromise','Different emergency pathway']],[SV],style='clinical_scenario')

q('SVC obstruction produces stridor, confusion and rapidly worsening upper-body congestion. Which approach is appropriate?', 'Urgent multidisciplinary stabilization and consideration of rapid endovascular relief when suitable',[
 ('Wait several weeks for routine clinic review','Severe airway/cerebral compromise is time-sensitive.'),('Assume all symptoms can be managed by oral diuretics alone','This does not relieve the central obstruction reliably.'),('Delay all support until tissue diagnosis is finalized','Immediate physiological danger takes priority.')],
 'Severe obstruction can require relief before the complete cancer workup','**Endovascular stenting can provide rapid symptomatic relief in suitable patients.**',['Cause, anatomy, clot burden and expertise affect the plan.','Airway management may be difficult and needs experienced coordination.'],'**Continue definitive etiological assessment after stabilization.**',['Do not assume a stent treats the underlying malignancy.','Plan anticoagulation or tumor treatment according to the actual cause.'],[['Threat','Airway/cerebral compromise'],['Rapid option','Selected endovascular relief'],['Later','Definitive disease treatment']],[SV],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Imaging shows catheter-associated central venous thrombosis causing SVC symptoms without an obstructing tumor. Which principle is appropriate?', 'Use a thrombosis-directed plan including anticoagulation when suitable and individualized catheter/intervention decisions',[
 ('Radiotherapy is the definitive treatment for every SVC syndrome','It does not treat a catheter thrombus.'),('A catheter must remain indefinitely regardless of infection, function or need','These factors affect management.'),('All patients require thrombolysis irrespective of bleeding risk','Intervention requires selection.')],
 'The syndrome names the blocked pathway, not the cause','**Thrombotic and compressive obstruction require different causal treatment.**',['Assess bleeding risk, extent, symptoms and device necessity.','Infection can change catheter management.'],'**Coordinate vascular, hematology and oncology care.**',['Escalate for severe compromise.','Use follow-up to assess symptom and clot resolution.'],[['Cause','Catheter-associated thrombus'],['Core treatment','Anticoagulation when appropriate'],['Device/intervention','Individualized']],[SV],style='clinical_scenario')
